* The idiom library, run rather than read.
*
* Everything `lib/idioms.bfm` offers gets used somewhere in `programs/macros/`
* except the four below, which arrived with the catalogue and had no caller.
* An idiom nobody runs is a claim about BrainFuck that nothing checks, so this
* program is their caller: it works each one on numbers whose answer can be
* read off the line.

@include "lib/idioms.bfm"

@var x
@var y
@var result
@var t0
@var t1
@var count
@var step
@var passed
@var flag
@var other
@var scratch
@var spare
@var out

* 3 times 3, which is the largest product a single digit can show.
@put(x, 3)
@put(y, 3)
@multiply(x, y, t0, t1)
@show_digit(x)
@show_digit(y)
@say(out, SPACE)

* Equal, then not.
@put(x, 6)
@put(y, 6)
@equal(x, y, t0, t1)
@show_digit(x)
@put(x, 6)
@put(y, 5)
@equal(x, y, t0, t1)
@show_digit(x)
@say(out, SPACE)

* Less, greater, and the same -- the third being the one a comparison written
* with a subtraction usually gets wrong.
@put(x, 2)
@put(y, 8)
@less(x, y, result, count, step, passed, flag, other, scratch, spare)
@show_digit(result)
@put(x, 8)
@put(y, 2)
@less(x, y, result, count, step, passed, flag, other, scratch, spare)
@show_digit(result)
@put(x, 4)
@put(y, 4)
@less(x, y, result, count, step, passed, flag, other, scratch, spare)
@show_digit(result)
@say(out, SPACE)

* And a swap, which has to move both ways at once.
@put(x, 1)
@put(y, 7)
@swap(x, y, t0)
@show_digit(x)
@show_digit(y)
@newline(out)
